const int MOD = (int)1e9 + 7;
int rec(int sz, int idx, string S) {
    if (idx == (int)S.size()) return sz == 0 ? 1 : 0;
    int ret = rec(sz-1, idx+1, S) % MOD;
    for (int i = idx+1; i <= sz; ++i) 
        ret += rec(sz-i, idx+i, S);
    return ret % MOD;
}
int distinctSubseqII(string S) {
    return rec(S.size(), 0, S);
}
```
</solution>

Sets are perfect for counting unique elements. Use a set to track unique results, as result strings don’t matter. However, keep an eye on the input size: If the sequence is too large, we can’t guarantee the modulo won’t spill over and overflow. Again, keep it simple, keep it tight.
